<Layout title="Identity, roles and phases" description="How we distinguish what a thing is from its classifications, the roles it plays and the phases it passes through, using practical property examples.">
  <h1>Identity, roles and phases</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A person can sell one home while buying another. A document can support several
    claims. A building can change without becoming a different building. A useful
    property model preserves these distinctions instead of turning every description
    into a different kind of thing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identity">Start with what stays the same</h2>
  <p>
    Identity asks when two descriptions concern the same thing, and what changes that
    thing can undergo while remaining itself. It is a business question before it is
    an identifier design. An address can change; several properties can share an
    address; a building, land parcel and legal title can be associated without being
    interchangeable. Matching the address strings does not settle their identity.
  </p>
  <p>
    For each candidate concept, ask what counts as one instance, how it begins and
    ends, and which changes preserve it. A property's sale does not necessarily create
    a new physical property. Splitting a parcel may create new parcel identities.
    The model must state the distinction and the context in which it applies, with
    examples and counterexamples that practitioners can challenge.
  </p>
  <p>
    An identifier supports that agreement; it cannot replace it. A database key may
    distinguish records without identifying the business thing across organisations.
    Conversely, one thing may have several identifiers issued under different schemes.
    Cross-context correspondence therefore needs an explicit identity argument rather
    than a shared label, a matching column or an AI similarity score.
  </p>

  <h2 id="distinctions">Four questions that prevent a confused hierarchy</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Identity distinctions in our modelling method</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Distinction</th><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Modelling consequence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Kind</th><td>What supplies the identity of these things?</td><td>Define the enduring thing and its identity criteria.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Subkind</th><td>Is this a permanent specialisation of that kind?</td><td>Consider genuine subclassing, with the inherited identity made clear.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Role</th><td>What is this thing participating as, in which relationship?</td><td>Model the bearer, participation and relevant context.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Phase</th><td>What temporary stage is the same thing passing through?</td><td>Model its stage conditions and the continuity of identity.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These distinctions, drawn from the selected modelling ODRs, govern how we model.
    Foundational modelling and OntoClean supply the analytical discipline; participants
    contribute the examples and domain judgments. Using that discipline does not mean
    importing an upper ontology. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision basis</a>
    identifies the normative records and distinguishes the required model from its
    current implementation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="isa">Use “is a” for an actual specialisation</h2>
  <p>
    In RDF Schema, <code>rdfs:subClassOf</code> states that every instance of the
    subclass is an instance of the superclass. It is a semantic commitment, not an
    instruction to indent something in a menu. “Appears under this heading”, “shares
    several fields” and “is stored in a related table” do not establish that commitment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our method applies a further discipline: distinguish a permanent
    specialisation from a contingent role or phase before admitting inheritance.
    A subkind inherits identity from its kind; it need not invent another way of
    identifying its instances. The useful test is whether the proposed distinction
    is inherent, and whether the asserted superclass statement remains true.
    We do not use subclassing as a substitute for the selected method's
    <code>roleOf</code> and <code>phaseOf</code> distinctions.
  </p>
  <p>
    A governed classification may be enough. Tenure labels, condition ratings and
    document categories require reviewed definitions, but they do not automatically
    require one OWL subclass per value. A SKOS concept scheme can provide stable
    codes, labels and explanations while the classified thing retains its identity.
    Classification and inheritance have different jobs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="roles">“Role of” keeps participation visible</h2>
  <p>
    Buyer and seller describe participation in a transaction. The same person can
    play both roles at once in different transactions, and can cease to play either
    while remaining the same person. A useful model records who plays the role,
    which transaction gives it meaning, and any relevant dates or responsibilities.
  </p>
  <p>
    Source-method ODR-0025 defines <code>roleOf</code> at the class level: the
    role type points to its identity-bearing type. This is project vocabulary,
    not a built-in RDF, OWL or SKOS relationship, and it does not automatically
    propagate types or properties. Keep that modelling annotation distinct from
    an individual participation record saying who acts in which transaction.
    The role view does not create a second business identity for its bearer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Role-specific information belongs with the role or participation that introduces
    it. A person's conveyancer for one purchase is not a timeless attribute of that
    person. Likewise, a document's evidential use depends on the claim it supports;
    the document does not become a different intrinsic kind whenever its use changes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="phases">“Phase of” describes change without losing identity</h2>
  <p>
    A phase describes a temporary stage of a continuing thing. The method
    calls the class-level connection <code>phaseOf</code>: the phase type points to
    the type whose identity continues through the change.
    The analysis asks what changes internally, what establishes the stage and how
    the same thing continues through the transition.
  </p>
  <p>
    A building's construction stages can be explored this way, provided the group
    agrees when the building begins to exist and what continuity means. But a label
    such as “approved” may instead record somebody's decision. Not every workflow
    status is an ontological phase. Keep an approval event, a status classification
    and an intrinsic lifecycle distinction separate when they answer different questions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="discipline">Rigidity, dependence and unity in ordinary language</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Rigidity</strong> asks whether membership is essential to the thing.
    A role is <em>anti-rigid</em>: something can stop playing it without ceasing
    to exist. A temporary category should therefore not be mistaken for an enduring
    identity merely because its label looks like a noun.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dependence</strong> asks what else must exist for the concept to make
    sense. Ownership involves an owner, something owned and an applicable legal
    relationship. A valuation depends on a subject and an assessment context.
    <strong>Unity</strong> asks what makes several parts one whole: a building,
    its component parts and a collection of buildings are not the same organisation
    of things. These questions improve definitions before any formal checking begins.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>relator</strong> is the method's term for a relationship
    with its own dependent business identity, such as a particular agreement linking
    parties. It is not a synonym for every RDF edge or every linking record. Dates
    or evidence can justify qualifying an association without proving that the
    association is a foundational relator.
  </p>

  <h2 id="review">Practitioners supply the decisive counterexamples</h2>
  <p>
    You can review this modelling without writing triples. Explain when the same
    person has different roles, when a title and a building cease to correspond,
    or when a status changes while the underlying thing does not. Ask whether a
    definition still works after a sale, subdivision, correction or change of use.
    These examples expose mistakes that a technically valid file can conceal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The examples here illustrate modelling questions, not approved SPDTF class
    definitions or new requirements for Property Pack data. The selected ODRs govern
    the method; domain review establishes the individual definitions and examples;
    implementation must then encode that agreement. Consult the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-rules">modelling rules</a> for the governing
    distinctions and the limits of each technical construct.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-patterns">Next: apply these distinctions with practical modelling patterns →</a></p>
</Layout>
